Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the constant-radius fillet between a cylinder and a cone, or two
cones, about one axis, along a circle about that axis, is an analytic
blend, a torus about the axis (mitcad#237).

OCCT's analytic blends need a plane among their faces (plane/plane,
plane/cylinder, plane/cone); between two coaxial surfaces of revolution
the blend was walked and approximated by a B-spline surface, and where its
contact passes over a cut-out of its face the walk rolled on the cut-out's
edge (the rim of a post that overhangs flats cut into it, the post rounded
up into a flaring underside) and failed.

- ChFiKPart_ComputeData_FilCoaxial (new): ChFiKPart_IsCoaxialPair tells
  such a pair, ChFiKPart_MakeFilletCoaxial makes the blend: in the
  meridian half-plane of the spine's point the ball's centre lies at the
  radius from both profiles along their normals oriented by Or1 and Or2,
  the torus is the circle its centre sweeps, and the section is the arc of
  the ball facing the edge, running from the contact on S1 to the contact
  on S2 with the torus's v rising (as the other analytic fillets have it;
  the torus's axis is turned to make it so). The contacts' circles, their
  p-curves on both faces and on the torus, the transitions and the blend's
  orientation follow the plane/cylinder fillet's rules.
- ChFi3d_KParticular: such a fillet (constant radius, no chamfer) is a
  particular case; ChFiKPart_ComputeData::Compute dispatches it.

Upstream: new code for a case that was walked before; the results agree
with the walked ones within the walk's approximation (the convex and
concave roundings of a post's conical top or flare, two cones, a bore's
countersink, whole and part revolutions).
